# TypeScript conventions

Applies to all `.ts` in this repo (the `sdk/`, `ts-scripts/`, `tools/`).

## Comments — only when necessary

Code should read without narration. Default to **no comment**.

- Never restate what the code already says (no "Sui network a deployment lives on" on a
  `Network` type, no "returns the id" on an `id()` getter).
- Don't add a `///`/JSDoc block to every type, field, or function. Name things well instead.
- Do comment the **non-obvious**: a tricky invariant, a "why" that isn't visible in the code,
  a workaround, or a unit/format constraint. One line is usually enough.
- Prefer a clear name or a small type over a comment that explains a bad name.

## Design principles (backend TS)

Lean on SOLID + clean-code practices (ref: https://github.com/ramziddin/solid-skills):

- **Single responsibility** per module/function; keep functions small and one level of
  abstraction. Use early returns over nested `else`.
- **Explicit types** on exported function params and return values. Avoid `any`; model a type.
- **Domain types over primitives** where it removes ambiguity (e.g. an `EntityKey`, not loose
  `id`/`tenant` args passed everywhere).
- **Builder, not executor** for on-chain calls: append to a caller's `Transaction`; leave
  signing/execution to the caller.
- One concept per file; group `use`/imports at top (no inline imports).
- Exhaustive handling of unions/enums (a `never` check or a `Record<Union, …>` that fails to
  compile when a variant is added).
